    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n30394" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Narnĭa</span></b> , ae. f., <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>an Umbrian city on the
				Nar</i>, now <i>Narni</i>, <i><span class="en">Liv. 10,
			10</span></i>; <i><span class="en">27, 9</span></i>;
				<i><span class="en">29, 15</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Tac. A. 3,
			9</span></i>.—Hence, </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Narnĭensis</span></b> ,
			e, <i>adj., of</i> or <i>belonging to Narnia, Narnian</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ager,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					31, 4, 28, § 51</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">equites,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Liv.
					27, 50</span></i>
			.—As <i>subst.</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">(sc. ager),</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					Ep. 1, 4, 1</span></i>
			.— In <i>plur. subst.</i>: <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Narnĭenses</span></b> ,
				ĭum, m., <i>the inhabitants of Narnia, the Narnians</i>, <i><span class="en">Plin. 3, 14,
				19, § 113</span></i>.</div>
